{
  "architecture": {
    "scope_and_inputs": "This cycle covers cycle i14-2026-10-s2 for Project SDLC and two selected Requirements: SDLC-FR-0201 (every stored event record, including its payload, must be permanently deleted seven days after it was received) and SDLC-FR-0202 (every stored event record, including its payload, must remain retrievable unchanged for at least ninety days after it was received). The UX decision is recorded as NOT_APPLICABLE: this is a back-end service with no user-facing surface. Both Requirements exclude backups. Both apply to every event record in the event store without exception, so they directly contradict each other. Any record aged between seven and ninety days would have to be both deleted and retrievable. No architecture can satisfy both as written. This document therefore defines one integrated design for the event store's record lifecycle: ingestion with a trusted receipt time, retrieval, and age-based permanent deletion. That design does not depend on which product decision resolves the conflict. The contradiction is recorded as a material product question and is not resolved here. The architecture does not choose either retention period, and it does not treat either Requirement as taking precedence.",
    "solution_structure": "The solution is a single back-end event-store service with three logical parts. The ingestion path accepts event records and stamps each one with an authoritative received-at timestamp. The retrieval path serves records by identifier or by query, and it refuses to return any record whose age is past the governing deletion horizon. The retention enforcement path is a scheduled lifecycle process that permanently removes records, payloads included, once they pass that horizon. All three paths share one lifecycle policy definition. That definition holds the minimum availability window and the deletion horizon, both measured from received-at. Because there is only one source for the policy, retrieval-time filtering and physical deletion cannot disagree. Once the product question is answered, the governing values are set in this definition. Until then, the design does not encode either seven days or ninety days as authoritative.",
    "components_and_responsibilities": "Ingestion Service: validates incoming event records, assigns the authoritative received-at timestamp from the service clock (never from producer-supplied time), and persists the record and its payload atomically. Event Store: the persistent store for record metadata and payloads. It is organized by received-at so that age-based deletion is efficient and complete. Retrieval Service: returns records unchanged, as they were persisted. It excludes any record whose received-at is older than the deletion horizon, so expired records are unretrievable even before the physical purge has run. Lifecycle Policy: one versioned definition of the availability window and the deletion horizon. It is read by the Retrieval Service and the Retention Enforcer, and its consistency is validated at startup: the horizon must not be shorter than the availability window. Retention Enforcer: a scheduled job that permanently deletes every record and payload past the horizon, including copies held in secondary indexes or caches within the event store, and records what it deleted. Clock Source: a synchronized time source used by both ingestion and enforcement so that ages are computed consistently.",
    "interfaces_and_data_flows": "Ingest flow: a producer submits an event to the Ingestion Service. The service stamps received-at, writes the metadata and payload to the Event Store in one atomic operation, and acknowledges the producer with the record identifier. Retrieve flow: a consumer requests a record by identifier, or queries by time range. The Retrieval Service reads from the Event Store, applies the Lifecycle Policy horizon filter, and returns the record unchanged, or not-found if the record is absent or expired. Not-found is indistinguishable for absent and expired records, so expired data is not leaked through existence probing. Enforcement flow: on each scheduled run, the Retention Enforcer reads the horizon from the Lifecycle Policy, computes a cutoff from the Clock Source, permanently deletes all records and payloads whose received-at is before the cutoff, and emits the counts and the oldest remaining received-at to observability. Policy flow: the Lifecycle Policy is loaded at service start and validated. A change to it is a governed release event, not a runtime toggle.",
    "data_storage": "Each event record holds an immutable identifier, an immutable received-at timestamp, metadata, and a payload. The payload is stored with the record, or in a payload store that shares the record's lifecycle, so that deletion is atomic. Payloads are never modified after write, which preserves the unchanged-retrieval property. Storage is partitioned by received-at, for example in time-bucketed partitions. This lets the Retention Enforcer delete whole expired partitions, which makes deletion complete and cheap and avoids leftover rows. Deletion is permanent within the event store, including derived indexes and any internal caches. Backups are out of scope under both Requirements' Non-Goals. Capacity must be sized for the longer of the candidate windows until the product question is resolved, because the volume retained differs by roughly a factor of thirteen between seven and ninety days.",
    "external_integrations": "N/A — The Requirements define no external systems; producers and consumers interact only through the service's ingest and retrieve interfaces.",
    "security_authn_authz": "Producers and consumers authenticate to the ingest and retrieve interfaces using the platform's standard service-to-service authentication. Authorization separates the right to ingest from the right to retrieve. Only the Retention Enforcer's identity may perform deletion, and no external interface exposes deletion or changes to retention. Changes to the Lifecycle Policy go through the governed release process, because altering it changes both the availability and the deletion guarantees. Expired records are reported as not-found exactly like absent records, so record existence is not disclosed after the deletion horizon.",
    "deployment_runtime_model": "The Ingestion and Retrieval Services run as stateless, horizontally scalable instances in front of the Event Store. The Retention Enforcer runs as a single-active scheduled job, guarded by a lease, so that concurrent runs cannot conflict. It runs often enough that physical deletion lags the horizon by no more than one scheduling interval. Retrieval-time filtering already makes expired records unretrievable, so that lag never makes an expired record retrievable. All components use the same synchronized Clock Source.",
    "failure_recovery": "If an enforcement run fails or is skipped, records past the horizon remain physically present but stay unretrievable because of retrieval-time filtering. The next run is idempotent and deletes everything past the cutoff, so missed runs are caught up automatically. Partition-level deletion is atomic per partition, so a partial run leaves no half-deleted records. An ingest write is acknowledged only after the record and its payload are durably persisted, so no acknowledged record is lost inside the availability window. If the Lifecycle Policy fails startup validation, the service refuses to start instead of running with an inconsistent policy. Clock skew is bounded by the synchronized Clock Source, and enforcement applies its cutoff using the service's own received-at values only.",
    "observability": "The service emits the following metrics: the age of the oldest physically present record and of the oldest retrievable record, records and bytes deleted per enforcement run, the time of the last successful enforcement run, enforcement failures, and the number of retrieval requests answered not-found because the record had expired. Alerts fire in three cases: the oldest physically present record exceeds the horizon by more than one scheduling interval, an enforcement run fails, or the oldest retrievable record is younger than the availability window while the store has been running longer than that window, which indicates premature loss. These signals provide the operational evidence for whichever acceptance criteria end up governing.",
    "migration_implications": "N/A — The selected Requirements and inputs describe no existing event store or data, so no migration is in scope.",
    "architecture_decisions": "Decision 1: there is one integrated lifecycle design and no choice between the conflicting Requirements. SDLC-FR-0201 and SDLC-FR-0202 cannot both hold for records aged between seven and ninety days. Choosing a period would answer a product question, which is outside the Architect's authority, so the conflict is raised as a material product question. The trade-off is that the architecture cannot be declared to satisfy both Requirements until the conflict is resolved. Decision 2: a single Lifecycle Policy is shared by retrieval and enforcement, and it is validated at startup. This prevents the deletion horizon and the availability window from drifting apart. The cost is that retention changes must go through a governed release. Decision 3: retrieval-time filtering is combined with scheduled physical deletion. Expired records become unretrievable exactly at the horizon without requiring real-time deletion. The cost is a bounded window in which expired data still exists physically, although it cannot be retrieved. Decision 4: storage is partitioned by received-at, with partition-level purge. Deletion becomes complete, atomic and cheap. The cost is coarser deletion granularity, so the partition size must be no larger than the enforcement interval. Decision 5: received-at is assigned by the service, not taken from the producer. This gives every record a consistent and tamper-resistant age. The cost is that producer event time is not used for retention."
  },
